ossup with two outlets calling it Lean D flip", "Polymarket prices Poindexter near 76%, well above fundamentals", "Polling is sparse, old (mid-August), and mostly from D-leaning sponsors"], "rationale": "The fundamentals point to a Republican-leaning district with an incumbent edge, but Miller's scandal, a favorable late-summer polling picture for Poindexter, and prediction markets pricing the Democrat near 76% justify a modest Democratic tilt. With the newest poll 51 days old and sponsor lean heavy toward Democrats, uncertainty stays wide and the race remains close.", "watch": "Fresh independent polls in October, or new developments in the allegations against Miller, could shift the race either way.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Newest usable poll is 51 days old; polling uncertainty inflated.", "Incumbent Max Miller (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Ohio's 7th District pairs a Republican-leaning electorate with an incumbent, Max Miller, whose campaign has been rocked by abuse allegations and a barrage of attack ads from both sides. Miller has threatened legal action over a Poindexter ad, and headlines have kept the scandal in national view. Poindexter has raised over $600,000 with outside support behind him, while Miller holds a large cash advantage.\n\nMost race raters call this a Tossup, though two rate it a Democratic flip, and Polymarket prices Poindexter around 76%. Available polls, mostly from August and often Democratic-sponsored, show Poindexter ahead by varying margins. The combination of a scandal-plagued incumbent, a strong challenger operation, and a favorable national environment gives Democrats a modest edge, but the district's lean and Miller's incumbency keep this genuinely competitive.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.6543, "margin": 2.32}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.7013, "margin": 3.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.7273, "margin": 3.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.7059, "margin": 3.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26",